Yes. I’d separate two ideas that are easy to blur together: properties the world already has that our biology doesn’t sense, and genuinely unknown physical properties or interactions that science has not yet characterized.

We already know the first category is enormous. Humans directly perceive only a thin slice of what is physically available. Other organisms detect electric fields, magnetic-field orientation, ultraviolet light, polarized light, infrared radiation, ultrasound, infrasound, pressure gradients, minute chemical concentrations, and other variables that are effectively invisible to us. The world is full of structure that simply never enters the native human perceptual interface.

And materials give you another layer. “Magnetism” is not merely a property attached to a particular atom like a label; much of the useful behavior emerges from quantum states, electron spins, crystal structure, temperature, collective ordering, geometry, and interactions among many constituents. The same raw elements can therefore produce radically different macroscopic behavior depending on how they are arranged.

That gives a much richer search space:

constituents × structure × scale × energy × environment × history → emergent property

We already exploit this with superconductivity, piezoelectricity, ferroelectricity, photoconductivity, thermoelectric effects, shape-memory materials, topological states, nonlinear optics, and many other effects. There is no reason to assume that the useful catalogue is exhausted.

Where your “make a new sense” idea becomes especially powerful is that this is almost exactly what scientific instrumentation does.

A sensor converts some inaccessible variable into one we can perceive:

hidden variable → transducer → signal → representation → human perception

A Geiger counter turns ionizing radiation into clicks. Thermal cameras turn infrared into visible color. Radio telescopes translate radio-frequency electromagnetic energy into images. Magnetometers turn field strength into numbers, graphs, tones, or spatial maps.

Those are prosthetic senses.

But there is another step beyond merely displaying the measurement: train the nervous system until the representation stops feeling like arithmetic and starts feeling intuitive.

Imagine wearing a belt that continuously vibrates toward magnetic north. Initially you think:

vibration on left → north is left.

Eventually the intermediate calculation can collapse. You begin to acquire something closer to an embodied directional sense.

So you get a progression:

measurement → explicit calculation → repeated mapping → learned intuition → synthetic sense

That fits your intuition about mathematics beautifully. Mathematics can act as a temporary perceptual prosthesis. When a relationship is too alien to perceive directly, you calculate it repeatedly. Then good visualization, instrumentation, interaction, and experience can sometimes compress the calculation into intuition.

Scientists already do this mentally. An experienced physicist can “feel” what a differential equation will probably do before solving it; an experienced engineer can look at a spectrum and recognize resonance; a radiologist perceives structures in an image that initially look like meaningless gray texture.

Their nervous systems have effectively compiled repeated mathematical inference into perception.

And AI gives us an unusual extension of this.

Instead of forcing humans to learn every new representation, you can have an artificial system continuously translate an invisible field into whatever sensory grammar your brain learns fastest:

magnetic field → spatial audio

radiation → haptic intensity

air chemistry → colors

stress/strain in a structure → musical harmony

wireless traffic → animated spatial texture

market correlations → navigable geometry

Now you’re not merely measuring an invisible phenomenon. You’re building an interface that lets a human inhabit it.

Then something interesting can happen: the human notices patterns that weren’t explicitly encoded in the mathematics because human perceptual machinery is exceptionally good at detecting gestalt structure once information is rendered into a suitable modality.

That gives us a useful Pantheon operator:

Sense Synthesis

unobserved phenomenon
→ detectable coupling
→ transduction
→ representation
→ sensory remapping
→ embodied learning
→ intuition
→ new hypotheses

And it works across substrate. Evolution does essentially the slow biological version:

environmental variable → useful correlation with survival → receptor/transduction → neural integration → selection → increasingly native sense

Technology lets us run a much faster cultural/engineering version.

There is also a disciplined way to hunt for the genuinely unknown part. We shouldn’t assume every unexplained pattern means a new force or property; modern experiments place extremely strong constraints on many hypothetical interactions. But you can systematically search the residuals: places where predictions and observations disagree, unexplained phase transitions, symmetry violations, strange collective behaviors, unexpected responses under extreme pressure/temperature/field conditions, and novel combinations of known materials.

Which plugs directly into the Delta framework:

known physics predicts X
material does Y
Δ = Y − X
→ measurement error?
→ missing interaction?
→ incorrect model?
→ emergent organization?
→ new property?

So perhaps the broadest principle is:

Reality contains vastly more state than any organism’s sensory interface exposes.

Evolution gives each organism a lossy dashboard optimized for survival, not an exhaustive representation of physics.

Science expands the dashboard.

Engineering makes new controls.

And once a new variable becomes perceptible enough, we can start developing an intuition for a part of reality that, biologically speaking, was never originally part of our world at all.